        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 7,
        "titulo": "Numbers from 21 to 100",
        "explicacao": "From 21 to 99 German reads the unit first and then the tens, joined by «und»: 25 = fünfundzwanzig («five and twenty»). «Eins» becomes «ein»: 21 = einundzwanzig. Be careful with 16 and 60 (sechzehn, sechzig) and with 17 and 70 (siebzehn, siebzig): they lose letters. 30 is dreißig, with ß. The hundreds add up: zweihundert (200), 101 = hunderteins. A thousand is «tausend». Phone numbers are usually read digit by digit.",
        "comparacao": "English says «twenty-five» (the tens come first). German says «five and twenty», like old-fashioned English.",
